{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=47398&jobTitle=+Lecturer+%28Teaching%29%2C+Integrated+Engineering+Programme+%28IEP%29%2FEFY": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching), Integrated Engineering Programme (IEP)/EFY",
      "description": "Job details:  Lecturer (Teaching), Integrated Engineering Programme (IEP)/EFY Skip to job title Skip to action buttons Close Study Research Engage About Give Work at UCL Home Home Search Jobs Application Process Rewards and Benefits Careers Community People Around UCL Temporary Work UCL Home Work at UCL Search UCL Jobs Job details UCL Home Work at UCL Search UCL Jobs Job details Lecturer (Teaching), Integrated Engineering Programme (IEP)/EFY Apply Now Ref Number B04-07761 Professional Expertise Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support Department UCL BEAMS (B04) Location London Working Pattern Full time Salary £56,431-£66,437​ Contract Type Fixed-term Working Type Hybrid Available for Secondment No Closing Date 06-Sept-2026 JD_Param11 Yes JD_Param12 Yes About us University College London (UCL) UCL is a multi-disciplinary university with a population of over 16,000 staff and 50,000 students from 150 different countries. Degree programmes are provided in Arts and Humanities, Built Environment, Brain Sciences, Engineering Sciences, Education, Laws, Life Sciences, Mathematical & Physical Sciences, Medical Sciences, Population Health Sciences and Social and Historical Sciences For more information see http://www.ucl.ac.uk/about. UCL East In our most significant development since our founding, UCL has expanded to east London. Situated on the Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park (QEOP), UCL East is dedicated to disruptive thinking and discovery across disciplines and beyond academic walls, to find solutions to the biggest challenges for future living. We are located in Newham, East London - one of the UK’s most socially diverse areas – and we want our staff recruitment to reflect this diversity. UCL Engineering UCL Engineering comprises ten academic departments that undertake research and teaching across a wide range of disciplines. Our scientists and engineers take discoveries from fundamentals to practice, producing innovative technological solutions to challenging problems. It is a dynamic and growing Faculty, with a strong vision and desire to use our talents to ‘Change the world’’. For more information see https://www.ucl.ac.uk/engineering. Centre for Engineering Education (CEE) The UCL Centre for Engineering Education (CEE) is a joint centre between the UCL Faculty of Engineering Sciences and the UCL Institute of Education. It aims to provide research and curriculum development to deliver improved engineering education worldwide. It is actively engaged in the engineering education research community and in using that evidence-based research to deliver innovative and high-quality student experiences at UCL and for external partners through collaborative consultancy projects. Engineering Foundation Year (EFY) In 2023, the UCL Centre for Engineering Education within the Faculty of Engineering Sciences launched a new course which reimagines the route to engineering study. The Engineering Foundation Year (EFY ), taught at UCL East, has a central aim of widening opportunities for students from under-represented educational backgrounds. Upon successful completion of the foundation year, students will go on to study one of a number of engineering undergraduate programmes at UCL. UCL Engineering’s Integrated Engineering Programme (IEP) Our Integrated Engineering Programme is recognised within and outside of UCL for innovation and thought leadership in engineering education. The award-winning Integrated Engineering Programme (IEP) at UCL is a radical model for reimagining undergraduate engineering education. It integrates rigorous disciplinary foundations with applied mathematics, authentic and enquiry-based learning, interdisciplinary project-based learning, and a strong emphasis on professional and transferable skills. The programme adopts skills-based learning approaches that prepare students to address complex global challenges through real-world scenarios involving industry, community, and policy stakeholders. About the role This is a 12-month fixed-term maternity cover position, commencing on 1st December 2026. The post-holder will sit within UCL Engineering’s Centre for Engineering Education and will work with colleagues across the faculty to support the continuous development and delivery of the Centre’s Engineering Foundation Year and Integrated Engineering Programme curricula. The Centre seeks to employ an education focused lecturer, ideally with both engineering and higher education teaching experience to lead and support the teaching within year 1 of the EFY and IEP. Industrial experience or experience of level 3 (A-level) teaching would be valuable. The Engineering Foundation Year has been developed to further our UK widening participation ambitions. Our students include mature students, students from low-participation backgrounds and first-generation students. It is an entry route into most of our Engineering undergraduate degree programmes. The EFY admits student that do not have the qualifications for direct entry to UCL Engineering and will support them, both academically and pastorally, to achieve their potential. The person in this post will support the integration, development and delivery of skills based and project-based learning education within the EFY curriculum. Additionally, they will serve as a personal tutor for a small number of EFY students, supporting students academically as well as in areas of pastoral care and their future career development. This would include supporting EFY students in their transition to their year 1 studies. The post holder will work with staff across 6 departments within UCL Engineering to develop and teach cross-faculty elements of the year 1 (level 4) Design and Professional Skills I curriculum within the IEP. In the main, the post holder will provide leadership of our Design and Skills curricula and teaching support at faculty level to staff and students in departments. About you The successful candidate should be able to demonstrate: Postgraduate Qualification (MSc/PhD) in an engineering science discipline or hold equivalent qualifications and / or experience Experience of Curriculum, Programme and/or Module Leadership and Development The ability to work independently, to lead and work successfully as part of a team, recognising when advice / input needs to be sought Experience of developing and delivering project-based learning and teaching at a level appropriate for Foundation and/or Year 1 Engineering students Proven experience of implementing innovative student-centred teaching methodologies Commitment to UCL’s policy of equal opportunity and the ability to work harmoniously with colleagues and students of all cultures and backgrounds Please see the attached job description and person specification for full details. Please also read the attached Candidate Guidance document. Important notes: All applications must include a supporting statement telling us, using examples, how you meet the essential criteria listed in the job description. Applications without a supporting statement will be rejected. Please do not attach any copies of certificates or research documents. If you are successful these will be requested at a later date. This role is not eligible for relocation expenses due to its duration. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. If you have any queries about the role or application process, have any technical issues, or need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, please contact the staffing team at faceng.facultyhr@ucl.ac.uk What we offer As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits some of which are listed below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Available documents Attached documents are available under links. Clicking a document link will initialize its download. download: TalentLink Candidate Guidance.pdf download: Lecturer (Teaching), IEP-EFY-Final JD.docx arrow_back Back to list Send job to a friend Print this page Apply Now Apply Now Information for Current students Staff Alumni Business Donors Visit Maps Library, museums and collections Bloomsbury Theatre UCL East Tours and visits Connect with UCL Jobs Media Relations Events UCL and London UCL Shop University College London, Gower Street, London, WC1E 6BT Tel: +44 (0) 20 7679 2000 Copyright © 2026 UCL Disclaimer Freedom of Information Accessibility Privacy and Cookies Slavery statement Contact Us Privacy & Cookie Settings We use cookies and similar technologies to enhance your browsing experience, analyse site traffic, and serve personalised content. You can manage your preferences at any time. Accept all cookies Accept necessary cookies Settings Privacy Policy : Learn more about how we handle your data and protect your privacy. UCL privacy policy Privacy Preferences Configure your privacy preferences for different types of cookies and tracking technologies. Necessary Essential for the website to function properly. These cannot be disabled. Learn more These cookies are required for: • User authentication and secure login • Maintaining your session while browsing • Processing secure payments • Preventing security threats and fraud • Remembering items in your shopping cart Analytics Help us improve by understanding visitor behaviour. Learn more We use analytics to: • Understand which pages are most popular • Monitor site performance and loading times • Track visitor journeys and navigation patterns • Identify technical issues and errors • Measure the effectiveness of our content Common providers: Google Analytics, Matomo, Hotjar, Microsoft Clarity Marketing Deliver relevant ads and measure campaigns. Learn more Marketing cookies help us: • Show you relevant advertisements • Measure ad campaign effectiveness • Limit how often you see the same ad • Understand your interests and preferences • Track conversions from advertising Common providers: Google Ads, Facebook Pixel, LinkedIn Insight, Twitter Ads, Amazon Advertising Embeds Enable third-party content and features. Learn more Important: These third-party services may set their own cookies that we cannot control. Types of embedded content: • YouTube and Vimeo videos • Social media posts and feeds • Microsoft Forms and surveys • Interactive maps and location services • Live chat and support widgets • External booking systems Save Preferences"
